The dipole moment of water is  

\[
\mu =1.85\ \text{D}=1.85\times 3.33564\times10^{-30}\ {\rm C·m}=6.17\times10^{-30}\ {\rm C·m}
\]

The electric field of a point charge \(q=+e\) at a distance \(r=100\ \text{pm}=1.0\times10^{-10}\ {\rm m}\) is  

\[
E=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{q}{r^{2}}
   =8.99\times10^{9}\frac{1.60\times10^{-19}}{(1.0\times10^{-10})^{2}}
   \approx 1.44\times10^{11}\ {\rm V\,m^{-1}}
\]

Reversing the dipole changes its orientation from \(\theta =0\) to \(\theta =\pi\); the energy change per molecule is  

\[
\Delta U = 2\mu E =2(6.17\times10^{-30})(1.44\times10^{11})
        \approx 1.78\times10^{-17}\ {\rm J}
\]

For a mole of water molecules  

\[
\Delta U_{\rm mol}= \Delta U \times N_A
                  =1.78\times10^{-17}\times6.02\times10^{23}
                  \approx 1.07\times10^{7}\ {\rm J\,mol^{-1}}
                  =1.07\times10^{3}\ {\rm kJ\,mol^{-1}}
\]

Thus the required molar energy is about \(1.07\times10^{3}\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}\).

